{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And on the eighth day, etc.</b> One must know why it was necessary to command this, seeing that the Torah already stated all the particulars of the laws of circumcision in parashat Lekh Lekha. And if it is to inform that one must circumcise by day and not by night, and also to inform even on Shabbat, as they of blessed memory expounded (Torat Kohanim; Shabbat 132a) ״וּבַיּוֹם״ — even on Shabbat — it is difficult: why did HaShem not record these particulars of law there, in the passage of circumcision that was written in parashat Lekh Lekha (Genesis 17:12)?" "Or perhaps it is because HaShem did not fix this particular in the commandment given to Abraham, so that a person should not think that HaShem relaxed Shabbat only with regard to circumcision for the sake of the patriarchs, who had not received the Torah and the stringency of Shabbat; but Israel, who were commanded by HaShem (Exodus 31:14) ״מְחַלְלֶיהָ מוֹת יוּמָת״ — would not set aside Shabbat. And even though they of blessed memory said (Ḥulin 100a) that the commandment was stated at Sinai but was written in its place, nevertheless there is room for an opponent to argue, for one should not learn to be lenient regarding Shabbat, and HaShem was not lenient in this except for the patriarchs who had not received the Torah. Therefore He commanded this particular to the children, who received the Torah." "<b>Further,</b> it appears that this is plain from the simple sense, for the commandment of circumcision stated in parashat Lekh Lekha (Genesis 17:12) to our father Abraham, peace be upon him — the matter is exactly as it was given from the mouth of the Gevurah to Abraham, without any addition of words to them after the giving of the Torah. From this point on there is no place for HaShem to command Abraham to circumcise even on Shabbat, for he on his own would do so — even though he observed the entire Torah in its entirety, nevertheless he would set aside Shabbat for circumcision, since circumcision is a commandment he was commanded in, while Shabbat is a commandment he was not commanded in at all; and if he did not do so, this would be, Heaven forbid, not a good thing he did. And if so, there is no place for HaShem to command him to circumcise even on Shabbat, and if He had commanded, the quills would break over Him: why was it necessary to command, etc.? Therefore the verse needed to command the matter to the children of Israel, who are obligated in the observance of Shabbat. Likewise, what they expounded there in Torat Kohanim, by the inclusion of the word ״יִמּוֹל בְּשַׂר״ — to circumcise even in the case of baheret — this commandment was not stated to Abraham for the reason mentioned." "<b>And it appears</b> that one may give a reason for the juxtaposition of ״וּבַיּוֹם הַשְּׁמִינִי״ with what precedes it, that it comes to give a reason for the delaying of circumcision until the eighth day, and that He did not command to circumcise on the same day, as we find in the case of מִקְנַת כֶּסֶף (Shabbat 135a). For this reason He wrote, after saying ״וְטָמְאָה שִׁבְעָה יָמִים״, <b>and on the eighth day</b>, etc. — to say that it is for this reason that He delayed the circumcision, and it is along the lines of what they of blessed memory said (Niddah 31b): so that not everyone be rejoicing while his father and mother are sad. And our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Devarim Rabbah 6:1), and this is their language: Why is the infant circumcised on the eighth? Because the Holy One, blessed be He, acquired mercy for him until he has strength; and just as His mercy is upon man, so His mercy is upon the beast, as it is stated (Leviticus 22:27) ״וּמִיּוֹם הַשְּׁמִינִי וָהָלְאָה יֵרָצֶה״ — thus far is their language." "And one must know: who revealed this secret — that in eight days he will have strength, no less and no more? And it appears that the strength mentioned in their words is what is stated in the Zohar (Tazria 44a; Emor 100a) — namely, that it is so that a Shabbat should pass over him and the vivifying soul that flows into the world on the day of Shabbat should reach him, as is known; and then he will be viable (בֶּן קַיָּמָא), and that is what the Sages of blessed memory called כֹּחַ הַחִיּוּנִי, 'vital strength.' And you will find that they of blessed memory said (Bereshit Rabbah 10:9) that before Shabbat came the world was unstable and trembling; once Shabbat came, it was strengthened and rested — thus far. And that is what they said: כְּדֵי שֶׁיִּהְיֶה בּוֹ כֹּחַ, 'so that he may have strength in him.' It further intends, in His saying <b>and on the day</b>, with the addition of the vav, to juxtapose circumcision to the command of niddah that precedes it, to say that this depends on that: if he observes the command of niddah it will add merit to perform the commandment of circumcision." "<b>Further</b>, the verse may be explained according to the words of our Sages of blessed memory (Tanchuma, Tazria 5), and this is their language: Turnus Rufus asked Rabbi Akiva: Which deeds are more beautiful, those of HaKadosh Baruch Hu or those of flesh and blood, etc. Why do you circumcise, etc. He brought him ears of grain and biscuits. He said to him: These are the deed of HaKadosh Baruch Hu and these are the deed of flesh and blood — are these not beautiful? He said to him: Since He desires circumcision, why is one not born circumcised? He said to him: Because HaKadosh Baruch Hu did not give commandments to Israel except to refine them with them, as it is written (Psalms 18:31): ״אִמְרַת ה׳ צְרוּפָה״, etc. — end quote. Now, the answer regarding the nature of the ears of grain was not sufficient, for they require a different repair after the deed of HaKadosh Baruch Hu, because what the ears of grain lack in their repair is for the sake of what is appropriate for human beings — they go and fix what is appropriate for them. But circumcision is the desire of Heaven; if so, let HaShem fulfill His will as He wills. And therefore Rabbi Akiva answered him that HaShem's intention was to refine Israel with them. Now, this answer is sealed and closed on the level of the intellect, grasped by Turnus Rufus, because his soul has no boundary for apprehending the inner intellectuality of divine knowledge to receive the plain sense of things. But for us, children of El Hai, it is for us to understand with clarity the truth of things. Also, when one considers the nature habitual in the world, which the Creator of all established — that every begetter or bringer-forth of a thing produces it in the pattern of the begetter according to its kind, and even in the details of the form of facial features, the image of this one resembles the image of that one — how could a circumcised man beget an uncircumcised son? Is not the portion of the foreskin in the newborn not present in the begetter? And one cannot say the reason is because the form of the begetter was originally in a state of foreskin, for what difference does that make since the foreskin was cut off and no trace of it is visible? And further, if so, Moses our Master, peace be upon him, who was born circumcised (Avot D'Rabbi Natan 2) — why did he beget an uncircumcised son, as it is written (Exodus 4:25): ״וַתִּקַּח צִפֹּרָה צוֹר וַתִּכְרֹת אֶת עָרְלַת בְּנָהּ״?" "<b>Indeed</b>, I will give you understanding in the matter of Elohim. According to what has preceded for us from their words, the aspect of the foreskin is an aspect of evil, for the deed of the foreskin declares an aspect of concealment, for the entire body is nothing but a receptacle for the soul, and the receptacle makes known what is inside it. And HaShem said that when Israel is circumcised, the foreskin being removed, they are stripped of the aspect of evil for which the foreskin is a sign — unlike the nations of the world, whose entire soul is the aspect of evil, which is the foreskin. And this is what He alluded to in His saying (Jeremiah 9:25): ״כִּי כָל הַגּוֹיִם עֲרֵלִים״. And it is a known matter that the creation of the first man was a perfect creation in the aspect of holiness, stripped of all evil, and therefore there was no aspect of foreskin in his form. And by reason of the sin, he drew foreskin upon himself (Sanhedrin 38a), and the aspect of the foreskin was born in him. And also drawn from this was the dominion of impurity in the woman, which is the blood of her menstruation, for this is the cause of her being as our Sages of blessed memory stated (Eruvin 100a; Ketubot 10a). Also, through the mediation of man's sin, an evil seed was sown throughout all his domain, and the earth brought forth its fruit in many kelipot." "And this is your sign: no person can benefit from wheat until performing many labors upon it beforehand and afterward, numbering ten — they are arranged in the chapter Kol Gadol said in Shabbat (73a), through the one who bakes, corresponding to the ten curses with which the earth was cursed, as stated in the words of our Sages of blessed memory in the holy Zohar (Pinchas 253b). And therefore, when HaShem removes the spirit of impurity from the earth, as it is written (Zechariah 13:2): ״וְאֶת רוּחַ״ etc. ״מִן הָאָרֶץ״ — to allude also to the aspect of the earth itself, then the earth will produce biscuits. Meaning, there will be no need for any one of the ten labors, but the earth of itself will produce biscuits of bread that require no preparation. And this is what Rabbi Akiva intended in showing ears of grain and biscuits, for this is the cause and the reason for the birth of the uncircumcised: the aspect of evil that adhered to the soul of the first man — its impurity fills the earth. And therefore, even though the man at the time he begets is circumcised, nevertheless the aspect of the formative powers that generate the seed from which the child is formed — their existence is a consequence of the earth, for man grows from its produce and from the produce of its produce, and from them the form of thought and deed is constituted in him. And since all these growths from the earth that sprouted testify to them that they are full of the aspect of evil in several gradations between the biscuits and the ears of grain, and since the seed from which the formation of the child comes cannot depart from this aspect, therefore the child emerges with foreskin on his flesh. And the second question of Turnus Rufus — if HaShem desires circumcision, why is one not born circumcised — his question is because he lacks knowledge of spirituality, and he supposed that the ultimate purpose of the act of circumcision is only what appears to the eyes in the body, not that there is anything in the inwardness." "<b>Or</b> it is possible that it was told to him and he knew that the externality in the material declares that which exists in the intellectual, and he supposed that HaShem would remove the stains and blemishes and purify the soul together with the flesh. And therefore Rabbi Akiva answered him a general answer, according to what he thinks in his mind: if he grasped nothing in his understanding except the aspect of the body, he informed him that the ultimate purpose of the matter is to refine their soul — not for what is revealed alone, for there is no difference between a man being circumcised or uncircumcised. And if his intention was to say that also in the spiritual realm, if HaKadosh Baruch Hu desires, He would remove the abomination about which the foreskin declares — he answered him as well that all the commandments that HaShem gave to Israel are nothing but to refine and to cleanse the dross that the primordial sin brought about. For all negative commandments are to remove the sicknesses of the soul, and all positive commandments are to illuminate the aspect of the soul, because through the primordial sin the lights of the soul were darkened, and through observance of negative commandments the blemish is removed — yet her light is still lacking, and by performing positive commandments her light shines, as it is said (Proverbs 6:23): ״נֵר מִצְוָה״. And this matter HaShem does not do, because man himself is the one who causes the evil — he sinned, and he shall bear his iniquity, which he clothed his soul with. When the first man sinned and all souls were dependent on him, all of them tasted the taste of sin, and it is upon them to drain out their sicknesses — and what place is there to say to HaShem: Remove from me the filthy garments that I clothed myself with! And see what I explained in parashat Bereshit on the verse (Genesis 4:7): ״וְאִם לֹא תֵיטִיב״ etc." "And further, if we were to say that HaShem would remove the abomination caused by man upon himself through his own folly — if so, there is neither reward nor punishment. And from this, the reason for the foreskin is on account of the primordial sin that adhered to the soul, and its offshoot is in the skin of the flesh. And HaShem commanded a man of Israel to cut from their body the aspect of evil, by which the aspect of evil is purged from the soul and it is purified. And by this may be explained His saying <b>and on the day</b>, meaning: because the preceding commandment and this one are two matters whose cause is one, which is the primordial iniquity — it is the cause of the impurity of childbirth and the cause of the foreskin of males. Therefore He commanded concerning both of them together: concerning the impurity of childbirth and concerning the cutting of the foreskin. Therefore He said <b>and on the eighth day</b> etc. And since the sin of the woman is greater than the sin of the man, the impurity departs from the man completely, whereas the woman — impurity is bound up with her, and she returns and becomes impure." "Perhaps the reason HaShem commanded on the eighth day and not before and not after, is that HaShem judges the judgment of his mother in it, for she must count seven clean days from the day she ceased [her discharge]; likewise the offspring is the thigh of his mother, and when he emerges from his mother's womb he becomes impure through the opening of the grave, and since circumcision is the revelation of His name, blessed be He, in man — as I hinted in the pericope of וַיֵּרָא אֵלָיו ה׳ — therefore HaShem commanded to wait until seven clean days have passed over him: ״וּבַיּוֹם הַשְּׁמִינִי יִמּוֹל״ and so forth." "<b>יִמּוֹל בְּשַׂר עָרְלָתוֹ.</b> This is explained according to what we have set forth, that the orlah is an aspect of the kelipah. Scripture hints here that three things must be performed in the commandment of circumcision, and they are: milah (circumcision), pri'ah (uncovering), metsitsah (suction). Milah is the cutting off of the orlah, and that is what it says — <b>יִמּוֹל</b> — meaning, he shall cut. Pri'ah is that which peels back the thin skin alone and does not require cutting, and this is what it hints in saying <b>בְּשַׂר</b> — meaning, he shall make it like flesh that has no vitality in it, and the skin of the pri'ah does not return and cover the crown again. And metsitsah is the defiling blood of these two aspects, orlah and pri'ah; since they are part of the body, they have a share in a person's blood, and it is intermingled in the totality of the person, and HaShem commanded to suction this blood because it too is an aspect of orlah — and this is what it hints in saying <b>עָרְלָתוֹ</b>." "And they said in the holy book of the Zohar (omitted in standard editions, see vol. I 62b) that there are four kelipot surrounding the aspect of holiness, in the sod of גִּנַּת אֱגוֹז (Song of Songs 6:11). And you will find that in a walnut there are four shells: (1) the bitter husk that dries up and falls off; (2) the hard shell; (3) that which separates between the sections of the food; (4) the one clinging to the food, eaten together with the food since it clings to it — and the aspect of this kelipah is not so detestable except when it separates from the food. And in the commandment of circumcision we separate from a person three aspects of kelipah: the orlah corresponds to the aspect hinted in the outer shell of the walnut; pri'ah corresponds to the shell of the walnut that breaks; metsitsah corresponds to the shell that separates between the sections of the food, which must be drawn out from among the food. And there still remains in him the aspect hinted in the clinging shell — and this is what they said, of blessed memory (Zohar I 78b), that a person until the completion of thirteen years is in the aspect of two orlahs, for HaShem set a limit for the trees of three years orlah and a person is עֵץ הַשָּׂדֶה (tree of the field) for thirteen [years], and from the fourteenth on all its fruit shall be holy, for praise." "<b>Further</b>, it may be explained according to what they said in Sefer ha-Tikkunim (Tikkun 24), that there are three levels in the aspect of this commandment of circumcision. The most exalted of all is the circumcision of the sons of the righteous, sons of El Chai; this circumcision is for them the revelation of His name, blessed be He, sealed in holy flesh, and it is the crown of Yesod. The second belongs to ordinary men, and this circumcision is for them like an offering before HaShem. The third is the circumcision of the sons of mamzerim — wicked men, hated by HaShem; this circumcision is for them like one who gives a portion to the serpent, dust his food. And see Tikkunim fol. 10. And the three the verse hinted in saying <b>יִמּוֹל</b> and so forth. Corresponding to the circumcision of the righteous, beloved of HaShem, He said <b>יִמּוֹל</b> — meaning, יוֹ״ד מוּל (yod circumcises), for it reveals in him the imprint of the Holy One, the letter yod inscribed in His name, blessed be He. And corresponding to the circumcision of the ordinary, He said <b>בְּשַׂר</b> — here He hinted the aspect of the offering that one brings in holy flesh to HaShem. And corresponding to the circumcision of those hated in Israel, He said <b>עָרְלָתוֹ</b> — for he gives to Samael the portion pertaining to the child, which is the aspect of the orlah. You thus find that HaShem commands circumcision whether for the sons of the righteous, whether for the sons of the ordinary, whether for the sons of the wicked — lest the court say, what do I care about a mamzer, to circumcise him when he is entirely full of orlah? Scripture comes to teach that even so, he must remove his orlah from him in order to weaken the power of the kelipah within him; for if he merits through his deeds, he will grow strong through the power of his good deeds and the good side will prevail and the evil portion will be nullified. And concerning this they said, of blessed memory (Horayot 13a): a mamzer who is a Torah scholar precedes a high priest who is an ignoramus."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {12 "The citation השמטות ח״א רס״ב appears to reference Zohar vol. I p. 62b in standard pagination; the page number רס״ב (262b) may reflect a variant witness or a different citation system."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:57:03.095839715Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}